# 1. GBrain is extremely relevant

GBrain explicitly uses the philosophy **“thin harness, fat skills.”** Its 34 skills are routed through a resolver; skills contain workflows, activation conditions, quality bars, and can call deterministic code where LLM judgement should not be trusted. ([GitHub][1])

More importantly, GBrain already has several things we should steal conceptually.

Its operational set includes `daily-task-manager`, `cron-scheduler`, `reports`, `cross-modal-review`, `testing`, `skill-creator`, `skillify`, `skillpack-check`, and a durable subagent orchestrator. ([GitHub][1])

Its `skillify` mechanism is especially relevant. A new skill isn't merely written; GBrain checks the skill file, scripts, unit/E2E tests, LLM evals, resolver registration, trigger behavior and overall reachability. It even detects routing gaps and unreachable skills. ([GitHub][2])

This is almost exactly what Moltwork needs for:

```text
WorkRun
   ↓
lesson discovered
   ↓
candidate capability
   ↓
skillify
   ↓
eval
   ↓
job-local skill
   ↓
more successful WorkRuns
   ↓
promote
   ↓
permanent specialist capability
```

So I would **not reimplement GBrain's ideas badly**.

I would either make GBrain a `Memory/SkillEvolutionProvider`, or adapt its strongest patterns into the WorkerKit abstraction.

[GBrain GitHub](https://github.com/garrytan/gbrain?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

