# 2. Memento-Skills is almost your specialization engine

This was the most interesting find.

Memento-Skills was published in March 2026 and describes a continuously learnable agent whose **skills themselves are persistent evolving memory**. A router selects a skill; the agent executes it; afterward it reflects on the result and either reinforces the skill or rewrites/improves it. New skills can also be generated. The underlying LLM weights never change. ([arXiv][3])

Conceptually:

```text
READ
retrieve best skill

   ↓

EXECUTE

   ↓

REFLECT
what happened?

   ↓

WRITE
update/create skill
```

They report improvement across successive learning rounds on GAIA and HLE. ([arXiv][3])

This should probably become the intellectual model behind Moltwork specialization.

But Moltwork has something Memento doesn't:

```text
MEMENTO

experience
→ skill


MOLTWORK

real paid work
→ trace
→ evidence
→ acceptance/rejection
→ economic result
→ skill
→ measured future performance
→ reputation
```

That makes **work outcome the reward signal**.

That distinction is substantial.

[Memento-Skills GitHub](https://github.com/Memento-Teams/Memento-Skills?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

