# 3. Letta fits underneath this beautifully

I now think Letta is more useful here than I initially did.

Letta currently describes itself specifically as a platform for **stateful agents that learn from experience**, with memory, skills, recurring schedules and persistent agents. ([Letta Docs][4])

Its memory blocks are especially useful because they're persistent but attachable/detachable. That means a worker could have:

```text
CORE BLOCKS

worker_identity
professional_rules
current_capabilities
current_goals


SPECIALIST BLOCKS

researcher_memory
accounting_memory
reddit_research_memory
customer_X_context
```

The same memory block can even be shared by multiple agents. ([Letta Docs][5])

So becoming a researcher can literally mean:

```text
worker
  +
research-profession-pack
  +
research-memory-blocks
  +
research-skills
  +
research-tools
  +
research-evals
```

Letta also supports client skills whose descriptions and paths are surfaced to the agent, meaning external WorkerKit skills can be made visible without making Moltwork dependent on Letta's skill storage. ([Letta Docs][6])

And this is particularly interesting for your idea of **selling complete agent configurations**: Letta already supports exporting/importing agents using its `.af` format. ([Letta Docs][7])

So a Moltwork asset eventually could effectively be:

```text
SMB Market Researcher v12

identity/profile
+ memory template
+ validated skills
+ tools
+ tool permissions
+ model routing
+ eval suite
+ examples
+ reputation evidence
```

Letta could instantiate the stateful agent while Moltwork owns the higher-level asset.

